# CUEMS firewall — opt-in nftables ruleset

The cuems-common package ships an example nftables ruleset that fences the
controller's WiFi AP (wifi0) away from cluster-internal services (NNG bus,
OSC, rsync, journal-remote). Operators joining the AP can still reach the
management UI (Apache 80/443) and SSH, but cannot poke at the cluster bus.

It is **not enabled by default**: lockout risk from interface-name drift
between deployments outweighs the autopilot value of pre-enabling.

## When to enable

On any controller that broadcasts the WiFi AP and where you want to keep
operator laptops (or their guests) off the cluster bus.

## How to enable

```sh
# Sanity-check the rules parse on this host
sudo nft -c -f /usr/share/cuems/firewall/cuems-wifi-fence.nft

# Drop into place
sudo install -m 0644 \
    /usr/share/cuems/firewall/cuems-wifi-fence.nft \
    /etc/nftables.d/

# Wire it into the main nftables config
echo 'include "/etc/nftables.d/cuems-wifi-fence.nft"' \
    | sudo tee -a /etc/nftables.conf

# Activate
sudo systemctl enable --now nftables.service

# Verify the table is live
sudo nft list table inet cuems_wifi_fence
```

## What the rules allow on wifi0

| Protocol | Port(s) | Why                                      |
|----------|---------|------------------------------------------|
| TCP      | 22      | SSH (operator login with org SSH key)    |
| TCP      | 80      | HTTP redirect to HTTPS                   |
| TCP      | 443     | cuems-editor UI                          |
| UDP      | 67-68   | DHCP for AP clients                      |
| UDP      | 5353    | mDNS / avahi name resolution             |
| ICMP/v6  | any     | Ping, neighbor discovery                 |

Everything else on wifi0 is dropped. Cluster ethernet interfaces are
untouched — they fall through to the kernel default (accept).

## If your AP interface is not "wifi0"

Edit the `iifname != "wifi0"` line in cuems-wifi-fence.nft to match the
actual AP interface (`iw dev` or `nmcli device status` to confirm).

## Rolling back

```sh
# Flush the table without disabling nftables wholesale
sudo nft delete table inet cuems_wifi_fence

# Permanent rollback
sudo sed -i '/cuems-wifi-fence.nft/d' /etc/nftables.conf
sudo rm /etc/nftables.d/cuems-wifi-fence.nft
sudo systemctl reload nftables.service
```

If you lock yourself out: log in at the physical TTY console with the
operator account, then run the rollback. Console TTY login is unaffected
by SSH / nftables config.
